 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over WebSocket connections that MarketBoard hosts itself: the server holds an open connection per viewing browser and pushes each newly committed event revision over it. No realtime vendor sits in the delivery path, so connection capacity, reconnect behavior, and socket-tier operations are the project's own responsibility. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed around this choice. The change store keeps a replayable per-event revision log and publishes each committed revision to the socket tier reliably, so a revision that commits is never only in the database. Delivery owns socket sessions: authorization at connect, per-event subscription, fan-out to subscribed connections, and the limits that apply when connection capacity is reached. Browser sync owns reconnection and catch-up — a browser that reconnects names the last revision it holds and is brought current from the log rather than resuming mid-stream.
 
